<GuideLayout slug="miami-dade-county-tree-removal-permit">
  <p class="note">
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> Miami-Dade County's environmental code makes it unlawful to remove a tree without a
    permit from the county, with exemptions for several single-family cases. Cities inside the county, such as Miami and
    Doral, can add their own rules. A dangerous tree on a single-family home can also be exempt under state law when a
    certified arborist documents the risk.
  </p>

  <h2>The general rule</h2>
  <p>
    Under Chapter 24 of the Miami-Dade Code (the county environmental code), removing or effectively destroying a tree
    requires a permit from the county's environmental regulators (DERM), unless an exemption applies. The county's rules
    apply in unincorporated Miami-Dade and act as a baseline in cities, which may require their own permit as well.
  </p>

  <h2>Single-family exemptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Existing single-family homes:</strong> trees in the yard of an existing single-family residence can be
      removed without a county permit, if they are not in a natural forest community and are not specimen trees. The
      exemption does not cover trees in county rights-of-way next to the home.
    </li>
    <li><strong>New home construction:</strong> limited exemptions apply for small lots where the owner will live in the new house, again excluding natural forest communities and specimen trees.</li>
    <li><strong>Dead trees:</strong> removal of a dead tree is generally exempt, outside natural forest communities. Confirm the details with the county.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Many species are on the county's exempt or prohibited lists. The county's tree and forest program can tell you
    whether a permit is needed if you send photos of the tree, a close-up of the leaves, the address and the trunk
    diameter.
  </p>

  <h2>Pruning is not removal, but over-pruning is</h2>
  <p>
    Selective pruning does not need a permit if it follows the ANSI A300 standard. Topping, or “hat-racking”, that
    effectively destroys a tree can be treated as unlawful removal. See <a href="/tree-trimming/">tree trimming</a> for
    what good pruning looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>Replacement</h2>
  <p>
    When a permit is granted, non-specimen trees generally have to be replaced with equal canopy, and specimen-sized
    trees need more, plus a contribution to the county's Tree Trust Fund. Ask what applies before you agree to a removal
    price.
  </p>

  <h2>The state exemption for dangerous trees</h2>
  <p>
    Under Florida Statute 163.045, a local government may not require a permit, fee or replacement tree for removing a
    tree on residential property (a detached single-family home) if the owner has documentation from an ISA certified
    arborist or a Florida licensed landscape architect that the tree poses an unacceptable risk. Keep the signed report.
    See <a href="/certified-arborist/">arborist assessments</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Cities in Miami-Dade</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/areas/miami-tree-removal/">Miami</a>, Doral, Miami Lakes and other municipalities can have their own tree
    rules, so check with your city as well as the county. If you live in an HOA, you may also need its approval.
  </p>

  <h2>Next step</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/contact-us/">Request a free estimate</a> and ask the professional what is usually required for your
    address. For rules across South Florida, read our <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">permit guide</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="text-base text-text-muted">
    This is general information, not legal advice, and rules change. Details checked in October 2026 against the
    <a href="https://www.miamidade.gov/resources/economy/permits/documents/tree-removal.pdf">Miami-Dade tree removal
    permit information</a> and <a href="https://m.flsenate.gov/Statutes/163.045">Florida Statute 163.045</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
